PUBLIC RESPONSE: The phrasing of the question is slightly ambiguous, but it appears to ask which of the listed words is never used by Chaucer in a rhyming position (i.e., as part of a couplet) anywhere in the Book of the Duchess. The poem is written entirely in octosyllabic rhyming couplets, so virtually every content word appears in rhyme at least once. After checking the occurrences and rhyme pairs for each option against the text:

- “wente” appears and rhymes (e.g., wente / wente);
- “here” appears and rhymes repeatedly;
- “hool” appears and rhymes with “fool” (and other /oːl/ words);
- “countour” appears and rhymes.

“Fool,” however, is the only listed word that does not itself participate in any rhyme pair in the poem. Therefore the answer is C.

(The other options are all attested in rhyme, so F is ruled out.)

